          <h2>The Verifier gate and the cyclic edge</h2>
        </div>
        <p>The most important hour of the build. This is the architectural claim you defend on camera: <em>nothing reaches the feed unverified.</em></p>
        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-h">app/nodes/verifier.py</div>
          <pre>@span(kind="GUARDRAIL", name="verifier")
def verifier(state) -> dict:
    ok, bad = [], []
    for c in state.get("ranked", []):
        if not c.get("exists"):      bad.append({**c, "reason": "venue not found"})
        elif not c.get("open_now"):  bad.append({**c, "reason": "closed right now"})
        elif c.get("distance_m", 0) > 50_000:
            bad.append({**c, "reason": "not where the reel claims"})
        else: ok.append(c)
    return {"verified": ok, "rejected": state.get("rejected", []) + bad}

def route(state) -> str:
    if state.get("verified"):        return "feed"
    if state.get("replans", 0) < 2:  return "replan"
    return "give_up"

g.add_conditional_edges("verifier", route,
    {"feed": "feed", "replan": "scout", "give_up": END})</pre>
        </div>
        <p>Note the span kind — <code>GUARDRAIL</code>, not <code>TOOL</code>. neatlogs renders and analyses guardrails differently, and a failed guardrail is exactly the kind of thing Detections and Investigate are built to find.</p>
        <div class="warn">
          <div class="eyebrow">Loop guard</div>
          <p><code>scout</code> must increment <code>replans</code> when it is re-entered. Without it the cycle runs until LangGraph's recursion limit throws, and your trace becomes forty identical spans instead of a legible retry.</p>
        </div>
